  "visualDNA": {
    "Overview": "The Zen sect was very successful, and therefore often imitated. Many of its innovations were therefore widely adopted by other Buddhist schools. Zenshūyō's characteristics are decorative pent roofs (mokoshi)  and pronouncedly curved main roofs, cusped windows (katōmado), earthen floors and paneled doors. Wood structures are relatively light, design light and orderly. All buildings are erected on stone podia and have either stone or earthen floors. Zen's discipline is strict and its rules many and complex."
